Localised Insect Bite Reaction
Common Infections and Afflictions
Pruritic papule or wheal, Central punctum, Localized erythema and induration

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-022
Reason for Visit: Sudden, intensely itchy and rapidly swelling lump on the lower leg.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient reporting an acute, rapidly expanding, heavily indurated, and intensely pruritic lesion on the lower extremity following outdoor exposure. The patient expressed extreme anxiety regarding the possibility of a venomous spider bite or an evolving severe systemic allergic reaction. Urgent home assessment is clinically mandated to perform an immediate dermatological and systemic evaluation, definitively rule out evolving anaphylaxis or rapidly spreading acute cellulitis, and aggressively suppress the localized histaminergic response. Delaying this assessment poses an unacceptable risk of systemic progression or uncontrolled severe localized distress, fulfilling the criteria for MBS urgent after-hours item numbers 585-600.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: Something bit me in the garden, and now this massive, rock-hard lump is driving me insane with the itch.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 4 hours ago following outdoor exposure while gardening in dense foliage. The patient noted a sudden, sharp pinch on the right lateral calf, which rapidly evolved into an intensely pruritic localized papule or wheal. The lesion has grown progressively firmer over the last two hours, presenting as a firm and indurated swelling with mild surrounding erythema and occasional mild localized warmth. A distinct central punctum is noted at the bite site. Multiple excoriation marks are present from vigorous scratching. The patient specifically denies shortness of breath, lip or tongue swelling, throat tightness, generalized hives, or dizziness, confirming an absolute absence of systemic anaphylactic features.
Functional Status: Independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High situational anxiety regarding venomous envenomation.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 128/80 mmHg | HR: 84 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused. Frequent, vigorous scratching of the right lower leg observed. No audible wheeze or stridor.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. Normal complete blood count without peripheral eosinophilia would assist in confirming the diagnosis in routine, chronic cases, but is not acutely indicated for this localized presentation.
Physical Exam: Dermatological examination of the right lateral calf reveals a 4cm diameter, intensely erythematous, firm, and heavily indurated wheal. A clear, singular central punctum is visible at the apex of the lesion. There is mild localized warmth, but no red streaking extending proximally (lymphangitis) and no fluctuance. Excoriation marks surround the lesion. The contralateral leg is entirely normal. Pulmonary auscultation is clear bilaterally. Oropharynx is clear with no uvular edema.
Environmental Observations: Patient’s gardening clothes are piled near the back door.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient has applied a generic antiseptic cream with zero relief of the intense pruritus.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Localised Insect Bite Reaction - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Acute Cellulitis - Ruled out by the extremely rapid timeline (hours rather than days), the intense predominance of pruritus over severe pain, the singular central punctum, and the absence of systemic fever or spreading lymphangitis.
Anaphylaxis - Definitively ruled out by the strictly localized nature of the reaction, perfectly normal hemodynamics, clear respiratory exam, and absence of generalized urticaria.
Tick Bite (Lyme/Rickettsial) - Unlikely as the primary acute presentation given the lack of an embedded tick, the rapid formation of a classic wheal rather than a slowly expanding erythema migrans (bullseye) rash, though tick exposure remains possible in outdoor settings.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The explosive onset of a severely indurated and intensely pruritic lesion generated significant patient panic regarding a venomous bite or severe allergic reaction. Urgent home assessment was essential to visually inspect the lesion, definitively exclude a systemic anaphylactic cascade, and rule out rapidly spreading bacterial cellulitis. Clinical findings confirmed a severe but benign, strictly localized hypersensitivity reaction to an arthropod bite. By providing immediate, potent topical corticosteroids and systemic antihistamines, the intense pruritus and swelling were rapidly controlled. This intervention and critical clinical reassurance prevented an unnecessary emergency department visit, satisfying MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Administered Cetirizine 10mg orally stat. Prescribed Betamethasone valerate 0.1% ointment to be applied thinly to the indurated wheal twice daily for up to 5 days to rapidly suppress the localized inflammatory infiltrate. Advised Fexofenadine 180mg daily for 3 days to control ongoing histaminergic pruritus.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically stop the severe itching without tearing the skin, apply a bag of frozen peas wrapped in a damp towel directly to the lump for 15 minutes whenever the urge to scratch becomes overwhelming. The intense cold chemically numbs the local nerve endings and immediately constricts the blood vessels feeding the swelling, providing instant physical relief without risking a secondary bacterial infection from dirty fingernails.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 3 days if the redness begins to spread rapidly outward or if the area becomes intensely painful rather than itchy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 immediately if you develop any difficulty breathing, swelling of the face, lips, or tongue, a sensation of your throat closing, or sudden severe dizziness.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a clinical hypersensitivity reaction. The specific insect species usually cannot be definitively identified from the bite mark alone.
Clinical Course: The extreme itch and hard swelling should significantly reduce within 24 to 48 hours of starting the steroid ointment and oral antihistamines. The localized lesion is fleeting in nature, generally lasting hours to a few days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if the lump develops a spreading red line travelling up your leg, or if you begin to feel generally unwell with a fever and chills.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: Your immune system severely overreacted to the insect's saliva, dumping massive amounts of histamine into the skin, which caused the hard swelling and agonizing itch. The oral tablet blocks the histamine systemically, while the steroid ointment acts as a direct fire extinguisher on the localized skin inflammation.
Actionable Steps: Absolutely do not scratch the bite. Scratching causes micro-tears in the skin, allowing bacteria from your fingernails to enter, which will rapidly turn this simple allergic reaction into a severe bacterial infection (cellulitis) requiring antibiotics. Wash the area gently with soap and water once daily.

SMS INFORMATION
